// Section: Dropoff — navigation, arrival/handoff, photo & PIN, delivery receipt/summary, alcohol-ID recognition surfaces.
{
  "$schema": "../../../docs/rules.fragment.schema.json",
  "screens": [
    {
      "comment": "#462 batch 2 — recognize-only idle/lifecycle + pickup/dropoff dialogs that shipped no viewIds and fell to UNKNOWN. No state.flow, no parse.",
      "id": "doordash.screen.dropoff_reminder",
      "priority": 51,
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Deliver to door of"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Deliver to door of "
          ]
        }
      ],
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextStartsWith": "Deliver to door of"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasText": "Got it"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.dropoff_navigation",
      "priority": 61,
      "redact": [
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_title"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Deliver to ",
            "Heading to "
          ],
          "normalize": "customerName"
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_address_line_1"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_address_line_2"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#986/#934: plainMask. After the 'Apt/Suite: ' label strip the masked token IS the bare unit number — the same bounded ~10^4 alphabet the fused 'Apt <n>' entries carry (#895/PR #927), sitting ABOVE the #889 short-token floor at 4 digits and therefore earning a 4-hex suffix over 65 536 buckets (~85% injective). Fielded 2026-08-01 on FOUR `dropoff_navigation` envelopes as 'Apt/Suite: [redacted:<4hex>]' (real fielded hex withheld — it is the brute-recoverable token this entry removes). The label is app vocabulary and stays; only the distinctness suffix is dropped, and it buys nothing here — this surface's name/street entries keep per-customer hashes, and a unit number is no one's join key. Capture-only (this node is never parsed), so the goldens are unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_subpremise_line"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_instructions"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#993: DoorDash's OWN arrival banner (`arriving_at_subtitle` = 'Arriving at' over `arriving_at_title` = the destination) can inflate while the dropoff workflow sheet is still on screen, and THIS rule wins that combined frame — so the customer's full street address shipped RAW in `arriving_at_title` right beside its own correctly-masked copy in `bottom_sheet_address_line_1` (fielded 2026-08-02 18:00:01, '<street>, Apt <n>, San Antonio, TX <zip>, USA'). The mask already existed on `nav_arriving` (nav-comms.json5) — it just did not exist on the dropoff-phase rule that can also claim a banner-bearing tree. Same entry shape as that sibling: whole-token, NO `normalize` (the customer-NAME canonicalizer would collapse an address to first-token + second-initial and destroy the mask's distinctness — addresses never normalize, per CompiledRedactEntry's KDoc). Unlike `nav_arriving` this rule is dropoff-phase by construction (state.flow `task:dropoff:navigation`), so the banner is always the CUSTOMER destination, never the store-ambiguous case #745 documents there. The sibling `pickup_navigation` restates a MERCHANT address and stays raw by design (#886). Envelope-only: this rule's require/reject/parse key on bottom_sheet_* ids and the CTA text, so recognition on replay is unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#886: the EMBEDDED Google-Nav maneuver cluster restates the destination. The sheet's own address lines are masked by id above, but the final 'arriving' maneuver renders the customer's FULL street address in nav copy ('<house#> <Street>, San Antonio, TX <zip>, USA will be on the right', fielded 07-26) and the preceding steps name the destination street — the one node family on this recognized dropoff surface still shipping the customer's address RAW. Masked whole and un-normalized (that canonicalizer is the customer-NAME key; an address token would lose mask distinctness). Nav copy has no analytics value and anchors nothing: this rule's require/reject/parse all key on bottom_sheet_* ids and the CTA text, so recognition on replay is unchanged. `roadNameView` (the road currently being driven) rides along — at the arrival step that IS the customer's street. The sibling pickup_navigation restates the MERCHANT address (driver-owned) and is deliberately NOT masked.",
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "primaryManeuverText"
              },
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "subManeuverText"
              },
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "secondaryManeuverText"
              },
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "roadNameView"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "Defense-in-depth (FIX 3): a modal-over-handoff combined frame that carries the multi-order-confirm's id-less first-name+last-initial line could be claimed by THIS earlier-priority rule instead of dropoff_multi_order_confirm, whose id-keyed redacts wouldn't cover the id-less name node. Same canonical name-shape SSOT as SnapshotRedactor.FIRST_LAST_INITIAL_PATTERN so the leak closes regardless of which rule wins the frame; hasNoId keeps it off id-bearing nav anchors.",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\s{0,8}[\\p{L}][\\p{L}'-]{0,20}(\\s{1,4}[\\p{L}][\\p{L}'-]{0,20}){0,3}\\s{1,4}[A-Z]\\.?\\s{0,8}$"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "normalize": "customerName"
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "task:dropoff:navigation",
        "modeHint": "online"
      },
      "reject": [
        {
          "exists": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasText": "Continue"
              },
              {
                "hasText": "Complete Delivery"
              },
              {
                "hasText": "Mark as delivered"
              },
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "complete_delivery_steps_button"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "branches": [
        {
          "require": {
            "exists": {
              "all": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_title"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "Deliver to"
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "require": {
            "all": [
              {
                "exists": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_title"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextContaining": "Heading to"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              },
              {
                "exists": {
                  "all": [
                    {
                      "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_arrive_by"
                    },
                    {
                      "hasTextContaining": "Deliver by"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "parse": {
        "as": "task",
        "fields": {
          "phase": "DROPOFF",
          "subFlow": "NAVIGATION",
          "customerNameHash": {
            "find": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_title"
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": [
              {
                "stripPrefixes": [
                  "Deliver to ",
                  "Heading to "
                ]
              },
              "trim",
              "normalizeCustomerName",
              "sha256"
            ]
          },
          "customerAddressHash": {
            "join": [
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_address_line_1"
                },
                "read": "text"
              },
              {
                "find": {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_address_line_2"
                },
                "read": "text"
              }
            ],
            "separator": ", ",
            "transform": "sha256"
          },
          "deadlineText": {
            "find": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_arrive_by"
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "stripDeadlinePrefix"
          },
          "deadlineMillis": {
            "find": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_arrive_by"
            },
            "read": "text",
            "transform": "parseDeadline"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.dropoff_photo",
      "priority": 62,
      "comment": "#598 F1: the customer instruction fields on this screen are all id-less free text — the handoff summary, a free-form delivery note (gate codes, custom directions), and the unit line ('Apt/Suite <n>'). None have a stable id or prefix, so the only complete mask is all id-less text (the require anchors — drop_off_workflow_host_fragment + the 'photo of drop-off' step_title — are id-bearing, so recognition on replay is unchanged). The 'Apt/Suite ' label is preserved for debug context; the standard handoff summary and any structural counters are masked too (accepted, capture-only).",
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#986: plainMask — the 9th surface PR #927 missed, because its prefix is the FUSED 'Apt/Suite ' spelling and #927 enumerated 'Apt '. Fielded 2026-08-07 on two `dropoff_photo` envelopes as 'Apt/Suite [redacted:<4hex>]' (real fielded hex withheld). Same argument as every other member of the family: the token after the label strip is a bounded unit number, brute-recoverable from 4 hex, and its distinctness is redundant beside this rule's own whole-node id-less mask below. Note the entry is ordered BEFORE the `hasNoId` catch-all deliberately (first match wins) so the label survives for replay context; without it the unit line would mask whole, which is stricter but loses the shape. BOTH spellings are enumerated in `keepPrefix` — the `find` already selected the colon render ('Apt/Suite' with no trailing space), it just had no label to keep, so it masked whole; `dropoff_pre_arrival` fielded both forms on different dashes and the family should not depend on which one a given surface happens to render.",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt/Suite"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasNoId": true
          }
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "task:dropoff:arrived"
      },
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "drop_off_workflow_host_fragment"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextContaining": "photo of drop-off"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.dropoff_pin_entry",
      "priority": 63,
      "comment": "#803: the id-less delivery-instructions body on this PIN-collect surface carried a customer full name + residence gate code + PIN past EVERY redaction layer — id-less (no viewId predicate), embedded mid-body (no whole-value name/marker match). The rule predicate vocabulary is per-node only (no sibling/parent relation), so the id-less free-text body cannot be isolated by STRUCTURE alone. These CONTENT-shape predicates instead mask the WHOLE offending node whenever it carries a gate code / PIN / address / whole-value name: a gate-code OR PIN fragment masks the entire body (embedded name included), which closes the FIELDED leak at the runtime capture edge. A bare embedded name with NO other PII anchor is the documented residual (the test-side pin keyword + SnapshotSecurityScanner shape + the #806 UNKNOWN CustomerTextMarkers path are the complementary controls). Mirrors dropoff_handoff's id-less shape set + the shared FIRST_LAST_INITIAL SSOT. Capture-only: never affects recognition/parse (this rule has no parse block; goldens unchanged).",
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "step_description"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasTextContaining": "gate code"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#803 F1/F3: bare 'gate 4821' (no 'code' token) — word-bounded, digit-adjacent (3+); the [\\s:#] separator class covers 'gate: 4821' / 'gate#4821' / 'Gate4821'. Masks the whole body incl. any embedded name.",
          "find": {
            "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\bgate[\\s:#]*\\d{3}"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#803 F1/F3: 'pin 4821' / 'PIN: 4821' / 'pin:4821' / 'Pin4821'. The [\\s:#] separator class covers the colon variants the first cut missed; dropping the trailing \\b closes the fused 'Pin4821' shape. 'pinch'/'opinion' stay clean — the next char is constrained to [\\s:#] or a digit. Aligned byte-for-byte with SnapshotRedactor.PIN + the scanner shape (three-layer parity, #362 class).",
          "find": {
            "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\bpin[\\s:#]*\\d{3}"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#886 (venue variant): this card's address block is id-LESS and label-less — `<line 1><line 2>` — so line 1 is named only by shape. A street line falls to the `^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S` entry above, but when the drop-off destination is a BUSINESS/VENUE line 1 is the venue NAME ('Sample Family Medical and Urgent Care', fielded 07-26): no id, no digits, no stable shape, and no preceding sibling to anchor on (it is child 0) — so it shipped RAW while its own city/ST/ZIP sibling masked correctly via the `\\d{5}` entry. That is a customer-LOCATION leak on a recognized surface. Anchor is the FOLLOWING sibling (#886's mirror predicate): the city/ST/ZIP line is app-rendered address chrome with a stable two-letter-state + 5-digit-ZIP shape, the only tight handle for a child-0 node. It covers BOTH forms of line 1 (venue AND street), so it is also defense-in-depth on the numeric entry. Masked whole, un-normalized (that canonicalizer is the customer-NAME key; an address/venue token would lose mask distinctness). Over-match surface is exactly 'the node directly above a city/ST/ZIP line' — in this card, only the address block's line 1.",
          "find": {
            "hasFollowingSiblingTextMatchesRegex": "[,\\s][A-Za-z]{2},?\\s{1,4}\\d{5}"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#889 F1: plainMask. The realistic population of this whole-node match is the city/state/ZIP line and bare 5-digit codes — a street line is claimed by the earlier '^\\d{1,5}\\s+\\S' entry and a gate/PIN body by the keyword entries above, both of which keep their hash. 10^5 values over 65 536 buckets is ~1.5 candidates per bucket, i.e. effectively recoverable, and the distinctness it would buy here is redundant: the street-address and name entries on this same surface already carry per-customer distinct masks. Fail toward privacy on a bounded token whose distinctness is redundant. Trade accepted: a long free-text body that happens to contain a 5-digit run also plains (it is masked WHOLE either way; its identity is not a join key).",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "\\d{5}"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#895 (+ the 07-29 F4 field finding): plainMask. The fused 'Apt <n>' unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet over the 4-char length floor, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (the #892/#795 oracle argument); distinctness is redundant here — the same surface's name/street entries keep per-customer hashes. The split label/value form plains via the id-less digit-shape entry when its VALUE is bare digits, and via the label-sibling entry below when it is not (#1039).",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt "
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039: the split label/value subpremise form spelled 'Unit <n>' evaded EVERY mask in this family — the fused entry above only sees a value LEADING with 'Apt ', and the id-less pure-digit shape entry only sees a value that IS bare digits, so 'Unit 4202' beside its 'Apt/Suite' label shipped RAW on two recognized envelopes (dropoff_handoff + dropoff_pre_arrival, fielded 2026-08-14 18:56 on the then-current build, i.e. two days AFTER the #986/#934 install). The value spelling is app/customer-authored — Apt / Unit / Bldg / # are all plausible — so prefix enumeration keeps losing that race; this entry anchors on the LABEL sibling instead (#860's `hasPrecedingSiblingText`, the same handle the 'Building Name' entries use), which is app chrome and stable vocabulary, so the mask no longer depends on how the value is spelled. Masked WHOLE (a value node has no lead-in worth keeping) with `plainMask`: a unit number is the same bounded ~10^4 alphabet as the fused form (#895/#986/#934), so a 4-hex distinctness suffix would be brute-recoverable, and its distinctness is redundant beside this surface's per-customer name/street hashes. The label node itself is vocabulary and stays raw. Capture-only — the node is never parsed, so the goldens are unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite"
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "\""
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#889 F1: plainMask. This id-less digit shape matches an Apt/Suite/entry-code VALUE AND the PIN keypad's digit echoes — the FIELDED #889 envelope was a dropoff_pin_entry frame carrying a single-glyph keypad echo, and pin_entry (priority 63) WINS over dropoff_pin_keypad (priority 109), whose own digit entry already declares plainMask. The #889 engine length floor plains 1-3 char tokens, but a COMPLETED 4-digit PIN clears it while spanning only 10^4 values against the 65 536 4-hex buckets (~85% injective), so the distinctness suffix would be brute-recoverable. plainMask drops it — the same #795 posture as the sibling keypad rule. The floor bounds LENGTH, this declaration bounds ALPHABET; they compose. Capture-only: these nodes are never parsed, goldens unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^#?\\s?\\d{1,4}[A-Za-z]?$"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "id-less whole-value first-name + last-initial — same canonical SSOT as SnapshotRedactor.FIRST_LAST_INITIAL_PATTERN / dropoff_multi_order_confirm.",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\s{0,8}[\\p{L}][\\p{L}'-]{0,20}(\\s{1,4}[\\p{L}][\\p{L}'-]{0,20}){0,3}\\s{1,4}[A-Z]\\.?\\s{0,8}$"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "normalize": "customerName"
        }
      ],
      "state": {
        "flow": "task:dropoff:arrived"
      },
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "drop_off_workflow_host_fragment"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "any": [
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "collect pin from customer"
                },
                {
                  "hasTextContaining": "ask the customer for the unique"
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.dropoff_pin_keypad",
      "priority": 109,
      "comment": "#795: the 'Enter PIN' 4-digit keypad MODAL of the PIN-required delivery confirmation sub-flow — distinct from dropoff_pin_entry, which is the 'Collect PIN from customer' STEP card, and matched by neither its 'collect pin from customer' nor 'ask the customer for the unique' anchor. RECOGNIZE-ONLY (no state.flow, no parse — mirrors dropoff_multi_order_confirm / pickup_wait_survey): the delivery already completes via the generic dropoff path and this transient sheet is a confirmation, not a lifecycle anchor, so recognizing it only graduates the frame out of UNKNOWN without any phase perturbation (and avoids a #603-class false-arrival off a sheet the dasher can open before arriving). Priority is behaviorally inert — the anchors ('Enter PIN' exact + the 'Before dropping off the order' body, both corpus-unique) match no other rule; 109 is simply the first free value across ALL doordash screen rules (51-108 are dense). PRIVACY (#795 Pledge): the entered PIN renders as raw node text — an id-less EditText carries the whole PIN and each pressed digit is echoed in its own id-less TextView. The redact masks every pure-digit id-less node to a PLAIN '[redacted]' (plainMask) — NOT the '[redacted:<4hex>]' distinctness form, because a 4-digit PIN (10 000 values) is reversible from 4 hex (65 536 buckets, mostly injective over so small a space). The PIN is never parsed/stored (no parse block); capture-only, goldens unaffected by the redact.",
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#795: mask the entered PIN — the whole-PIN EditText and each echoed single-digit TextView are id-less pure-digit nodes. plainMask drops the distinctness hash (a bounded PIN is recoverable from 4 hex). The rule's require anchors are non-digit text nodes, so masking these leaves replay recognition unchanged.",
          "find": {
            "all": [
              {
                "hasTextMatchesRegex": "^\\d{1,6}$"
              },
              {
                "hasNoId": true
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        }
      ],
      "require": {
        "all": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "drop_off_workflow_host_fragment"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasText": "Enter PIN"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasTextContaining": "Before dropping off the order"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.dropoff_pin_intro",
      "priority": 112,
      "redact": [
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt (the FIX 3 precedent): this dropoff-phase surface can win a frame that STILL carries DoorDash's nav arrival banner — `dropoff_navigation`'s own reject (Continue / Complete Delivery / Mark as delivered / complete_delivery_steps_button) hands the arrival-MOMENT frame to whichever arrived-phase rule matches, and nav_arriving rejects bottom-sheet frames, so neither of the two rules that already masked `arriving_at_title` covers it. The node is id-BEARING, so the id-less name/address entries elsewhere in this block structurally cannot reach it (`hasNoId` conjunct). Whole token, no normalize — it is a customer ADDRESS on every dropoff-phase surface (see dropoff_navigation for the full reasoning). Inert on any frame without the node; `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` pins that EVERY rule in this file carries it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        }
      ],
      "comment": "#795: the informational bottom sheet that precedes the PIN keypad — 'You'll need a PIN from the customer to confirm delivery' + 'To protect your Dasher rating...'. RECOGNIZE-ONLY (no state.flow, no parse — mirrors dropoff_multi_order_confirm): a pre-arrival advisory sheet, not a lifecycle anchor; recognizing it only graduates the frame out of UNKNOWN. The sheet's OWN copy needs no mask — it carries only generic DoorDash text, no customer name/address/PIN (verified against both captured frames, text and contentDescription); its single redact entry is the #993 nav-banner belt, which is inert on any frame that does not carry the banner. Anchors are corpus-unique; 112 is the next free screen priority after 109 (behaviorally inert).",
